Chapter: EdAliasTest

 ge-add and ge-double are the only Ed25519 operations that fail on the
 cross lanes, and the thing they do that fe-mul does not is rely on
 fe-add and fe-sub writing into their first argument and returning it.
 That contract has two halves: list-set-at writes in place and returns
 the same list, and arguments evaluate left to right. This file states
 both halves as answers a person can check.

 The expected values are the semantics the Developer's Guide records,
 not numbers read back off a backend.

Section: Mutating helpers

 The fe-add shape: write into the first argument, return it.

  add-into : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  add-into (a) (b) = add-loop a b 0

  add-loop : List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  add-loop (a) (b) (i) =
    if i == 4 then a
    else let v = list-set-at a i (list-at a i + list-at b i)
    in add-loop v b (i + 1)

  copy4 : List Integer -> List Integer
  copy4 (a) = copy-loop a 0 [0, 0, 0, 0]

  copy-loop : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  copy-loop (a) (i) (out) =
    if i == 4 then out
    else copy-loop a (i + 1) (list-set-at out i (list-at a i))

  sum4 : List Integer -> Integer
  sum4 (xs) = list-at xs 0 + list-at xs 1 + list-at xs 2 + list-at xs 3

 Appends its tag to a shared log and advances the log's cursor, which
 lives in slot 0. Two of these in one call record the order the two
 argument positions were evaluated in.

  bump : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  bump (log) (tag) =
    let cur = list-at log 0
    in let l2 = list-set-at log (cur + 1) tag
    in let l3 = list-set-at l2 0 (cur + 1)
    in tag

  pair : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  pair (a) (b) = a * 0 + b * 0

Section: Reports

 list-set-at writes in place and hands back the same list, so the
 original binding sees the write. 1+2+3+4 becomes 11+2+3+4.

  r1 : Text =
    "inplace=" & show (let xs = copy4 [1, 2, 3, 4]
                       in let ys = list-set-at xs 0 11
                       in sum4 xs)

 And the returned list is the same object, not a copy, so both names
 report the same total.

  r2 : Text =
    "same-object=" & show (let xs = copy4 [1, 2, 3, 4]
                           in let ys = list-set-at xs 0 11
                           in sum4 ys - sum4 xs)

 The fe-add contract: adding a copy of a list to itself doubles it and
 leaves the source alone. 1+2+3+4 = 10, doubled is 20, source still 10.

  r3 : Text =
    "add-into-sum=" & show (let src = copy4 [1, 2, 3, 4]
                            in sum4 (add-into (copy4 src) src))

  r4 : Text =
    "src-untouched=" & show (let src = copy4 [1, 2, 3, 4]
                             in let d = add-into (copy4 src) src
                             in sum4 src)

 Without the copy, add-into mutates its first argument while reading
 its second, which is the same list. Each limb is read after the
 earlier limbs were written, so this is the aliased shape ge-double
 relies on being left to right.

  r5 : Text =
    "aliased=" & show (let src = copy4 [1, 2, 3, 4]
                       in sum4 (add-into src src))

 Argument order, in one call with two mutating argument positions.
 Left to right records 7 then 9 and answers 79; right to left answers
 97. fe-add and fe-sub depend on the former.

  r6 : Text =
    "argorder=" & show (let log = copy4 [0, 0, 0, 0]
                        in let z = pair (bump log 7) (bump log 9)
                        in list-at log 1 * 10 + list-at log 2)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni r1
    print-line-uni r2
    print-line-uni r3
    print-line-uni r4
    print-line-uni r5
    print-line-uni r6
  end
